<interrogationes unitas="http">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make an HTTPS request and read the response body?
    <responsum symbolum="http_petitio_creare http_petitio_caput_addere http_petitio_corpus_ponere http_exsequi HttpResultus">Build
      the request with http_petitio_creare(piscina, HTTP_POST, url)
      (NIHIL if the URL has no "://"), add headers with
      http_petitio_caput_addere and a body with
      http_petitio_corpus_ponere (copied), then call
      http_exsequi(petitio, piscina). On res.successus, read
      res.responsum->status and res.responsum->corpus. Host,
      Content-Length (only when the body is not empty) and
      "Connection: close" are added for you. An https URL goes through
      tls.h; anything else is plain TCP. A request holds at most LXIV
      headers (http_petitio_caput_addere drops further ones silently),
      and the automatic Host header never carries a non-default
      port.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I treat res.successus as "the server said 200"?
    <responsum symbolum="http_exsequi http_error_descriptio HttpError">No.
      successus means the exchange worked and the response parsed: a
      404 or 500 still comes back with successus VERUM, so check
      res.responsum->status yourself. When successus is FALSUM,
      res.responsum is NIHIL and res.error is the HttpError (CONNEXIO,
      TIMEOUT, IO, PARSE, URL, REDIRECTIO), with a description in
      res.error_descriptio; http_error_descriptio names the
      code.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass responsum->corpus.datum to a function that expects a C string?
    <responsum symbolum="http_exsequi http_exsequi_rivus chorda_ut_cstr">Not
      safely. Whether a NUL follows the body depends on the path: the
      Content-Length and read-to-EOF paths of http_exsequi copy it with
      a trailing NUL, but a chunked body and every body from
      http_exsequi_rivus are not guaranteed to be terminated, and an
      empty body has datum NIHIL. Use corpus.mensura, or chorda_ut_cstr when you really
      need a C string.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I set a timeout on a request?
    <responsum symbolum="http_petitio_tempus_ponere http_exsequi" via="lib/http.worklog.md">http_petitio_tempus_ponere(petitio,
      ms) before executing; 0 keeps the default of XXX seconds. It
      applies to plain TCP and, since 2026-10-07, to TLS as well. On
      the plain path it becomes SO_RCVTIMEO and SO_SNDTIMEO on the
      socket after connect, so it bounds each wait for data, not the
      whole request (a server that trickles bytes never times out),
      and does not bound DNS or the connect itself. When
      it expires http_exsequi returns HTTP_ERROR_TIMEOUT and throws away
      any partial body on purpose: a truncated body presented as success
      is worse than an honest error. For the same reason a body shorter
      than its Content-Length, or a chunked body without the final 0
      chunk, is HTTP_ERROR_IO (RFC 9112 6.3). http_exsequi always sends
      "Connection: close", reads until the server closes and has no
      size cap; http_exsequi_rivus stops at the end of the message
      instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I stream a response body as it arrives, for example a model's output?
    <responsum symbolum="http_exsequi_rivus HttpRivus http_vectura_rivi_exsequi">Call
      http_exsequi_rivus(petitio, rivus, datum, piscina) with an
      HttpRivus callback (datum, octeti, n). The callback receives the
      decoded body bytes (Content-Length, chunked or until close) as
      they arrive, but ONLY for a 2xx status; the whole body is also
      collected in responsum->corpus (up to HTTP_LECTOR_CORPUS_MAXIMUM,
      XVI * M * M bytes; past that the call fails with
      HTTP_ERROR_PARSE "corpus nimium"). It stops reading as soon as the message is complete
      instead of waiting for the server to close. Use
      HttpVecturaRivi and http_vectura_rivi_exsequi where tests must
      replace the network.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use http_exsequi_rivus without a callback, as a drop-in for http_exsequi?
    <responsum symbolum="http_exsequi_rivus http_exsequi http_lector_creare">Yes:
      pass NIHIL as the rivus and the reader only collects the body
      into responsum->corpus. The differences: it stops reading at the
      end of the message (http_exsequi waits for the server to close),
      it understands chunk extensions ("5;name=value"), which the
      older chunked decoder in http_exsequi misreads (it copies the
      extension bytes as data and can then report success with a wrong
      body), and it caps the body at XVI * M * M bytes where
      http_exsequi has no cap. Its body is not guaranteed to be
      NUL-terminated; malformed framing comes back as HTTP_ERROR_PARSE,
      a body cut short at EOF as HTTP_ERROR_IO.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse an HTTP response from bytes I read myself, for example from a reactor callback?
    <responsum symbolum="http_lector_creare http_lector_adicere http_lector_finire http_lector_responsum http_lector_error">Use
      the incremental reader, which does no I/O:
      http_lector_creare(piscina, methodus, rivus, datum), then feed
      each chunk to http_lector_adicere until it returns
      HTTP_LECTOR_FINITUS or HTTP_LECTOR_ERROR; chunk boundaries may
      fall anywhere. Call http_lector_finire at EOF (it finishes a
      read-until-close body, otherwise reports "corpus truncatum").
      http_lector_responsum is NIHIL until the headers are complete;
      http_lector_error gives the reason. Limits: LXIV * M bytes of
      headers, XVI * M * M bytes of body; bytes after the end of the
      message are ignored. An interim 1xx response (100 Continue, 103
      Early Hints) is taken as the final response: the reader finishes
      on it, so the real response that follows is ignored (http_exsequi
      likewise reports the first status line it sees).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I rely on http_exsequi_cum_redirectionibus to follow redirects?
    <responsum symbolum="http_exsequi_cum_redirectionibus http_exsequi">Not
      yet: it has no test and no caller. It follows only 301, 302, 307
      and 308 (not 303); it keeps the method on 301 and 302 but drops
      the body; it copies every original header except Host to the new
      URL, Authorization included, even to another host; and the new
      request loses the timeout set with http_petitio_tempus_ponere. A
      one-character Location such as "/" also makes its absolute-URL
      scan run past the end of the string, and a relative Location
      that contains "://" anywhere (say "/login?next=https://x/") is
      taken as absolute, so the request goes to host x. Handle 3xx
      yourself with http_exsequi for now.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what URL forms does http_petitio_creare understand?
    <responsum symbolum="http_petitio_creare http_petitio_visus">scheme://host[:port][/path?query].
      The scheme is compared case-sensitively: only "https" gets TLS
      and port 443, every other scheme (including "HTTPS" and "ftp")
      gets plain TCP on port 80. There is no userinfo, no IPv6 literal
      and no check that the port is below 65536, and the path is sent
      as written (no escaping). The host ends only at ':' or '/', so
      "http://host?q=1" makes "host?q=1" the host: write
      "http://host/?q=1". http_petitio_visus shows how a URL was
      split.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test code that makes HTTP calls without touching the network?
    <responsum symbolum="HttpVectura http_vectura_ordinaria http_vectura_exsequi http_petitio_visus">Take
      an HttpVectura {exsequi, datum} instead of calling http_exsequi
      directly, and send through http_vectura_exsequi. Production
      passes http_vectura_ordinaria() (which is http_exsequi); a test
      passes its own exsequi function that inspects the request with
      http_petitio_visus (method, scheme, host, port, path, body,
      headers, timeout) and returns a scripted HttpResultus. A vectura
      whose exsequi is NIHIL gives HTTP_ERROR_CONNEXIO, never a
      crash. herbarium wraps a vectura the same way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I parse incoming requests on the server side?
    <responsum symbolum="http_parser_creare_cum_limitibus http_parser_adicere HttpParseResultus http_parser_reliquiae">Create
      a parser with http_parser_creare_cum_limitibus(piscina,
      petitio_maxima, uri_maxima) (0 = M * M and VIII * M bytes) and feed each
      read to http_parser_adicere. While res.successus is VERUM and
      res.completa FALSUM, wait for more data; on completa read
      res.petitio (via, quaestio, capita, corpus, keep_alive). On
      failure answer with res.status_suggestus: 400 (bad line or
      version, duplicate Content-Length, Content-Length with
      Transfer-Encoding), 411 (chunked request bodies are refused), 413,
      414, 501 (unknown method). Bare LF line ends are accepted.
      http_parser_reliquiae counts pipelined bytes left over; hospitium
      closes the connection after answering in that case. Stop feeding
      after the first failure: a later http_parser_adicere on a failed
      parser returns successus VERUM and completa FALSUM, and data fed
      after completa is dropped. Only the first LXIV headers are kept,
      and the Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding and Connection checks
      run only on those, so a Content-Length after the 64th header is
      ignored (no body, no 400).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does hospitium create a new parser for every request instead of calling http_parser_reset?
    <responsum symbolum="http_parser_reset http_parser_creare_cum_limitibus piscina_reficere" via="lib/hospitium.c">Its
      parser lives in the connection's piscina, which is rolled back
      with piscina_reficere after each request; the parser's buffer and
      request struct would be dangling after the rollback. Note also
      that http_parser_reset keeps the limits but empties the buffer,
      so any pipelined bytes still in it are lost, and the reset reuses
      the same HttpPetitioServeri, overwriting a request you still
      hold.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I serialize a response on the server, and who writes Content-Length?
    <responsum symbolum="http_responsum_serialize_cum_conexione http_responsum_serialize http_status_descriptio">http_responsum_serialize_cum_conexione(responsum,
      keep_alive, piscina) adds "Connection: keep-alive" or "close";
      http_responsum_serialize adds no Connection header. Both own the
      framing: they always write Content-Length (even 0) except for
      1xx, 204 and 304, and skip any Content-Length or Connection
      header you put in capita. The reason phrase comes from
      http_status_descriptio(status), not from
      responsum->status_descriptio, so an unlisted code (206, 418,
      ...) goes out as "Unknown". A body you set is written even for
      1xx, 204 and 304, where no Content-Length frames it, so leave
      corpus empty for those.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
